  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 AM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nA persistent H5 ridge continues to linger over the Four Corners \nregion. This ridge has been contracting over the past 48 hours, \nallowing weak mid-level energy to assist with isolated shower and \nthunderstorm development along the seabreeze this afternoon. As the \nweek progresses, the aforementioned ridge is expected to expand \nheading into the weekend and an easterly wave looks to round the \nsouthern periphery of the Bermuda High into our eastern counties on \nSunday. PoPs will be kept below 15% across the CWA into Saturday to \naccount for seabreeze convection each afternoon. Can't rule out a 20-\n25% chance around the Victoria Crossroads on Sunday and Monday as \nmoisture from the easterly wave works inland.  \n\nSeasonably warm conditions stretch on through the forecast period, \nwith high temperatures largely in the triple digits west of US-281 \nand and 90s elsewhere. Expect heat indices to consistently range \nbetween 100 to 110 degrees areawide, but remaining at or above 110\ndegrees for multiple hours would be isolated. Even though we may \nremain below heat advisory criteria, please remember to drink \nplenty of water and take frequent breaks in shade or A/C as we are\nentering the hottest time of the year climatologically in South \nTexas.",
